{"/api/discogs/artist/10991/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"10991","image":"https://i.discogs.com/Cde475tpcDnnAzERdMmnXz8HpOFQvk0Ejp7FVYfarW8/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:554/w:554/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTEwOTkx/LTE3NzU0OTQyNjYt/NzIwNS5wbmc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/S_vIWuZfuQa71tuFwdotMdTlAO1NNmwHmMpK-PRLFSg/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTEwOTkx/LTE3NzU0OTQyNjYt/NzIwNS5wbmc.jpeg","profile":"Artis Leon Ivey Jr., better known by the stage name Coolio, was an American musician, rapper, actor, and record producer. He is best known for the song \"Gangsta's Paradise\".\r\n\r\nDied on Fentanyl Overdose at age 59.\r\n\r\nBorn: August 1, 1963, Monessen, Pennsylvania.\r\nDied: September 28, 2022, Los Angeles, California.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:10991","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":633285,"name":"Artis Ivey"}],"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":["Bay Coolio","Colio","Collio","Cool:o","Coolie","Julio","Koolio","קוליו","クーリオ","酷力歐","酷立歐"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/10991-Coolio","type":"artist","urls":["http://www.coolio.com/","http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coolio","https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coolio","https://www.instagram.com/coolio/?hl=en"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/1398/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"1398","image":"https://i.discogs.com/N3QGS9dU_rQuFebxlDgOkghWmhU-yPViXQAis7q2yYs/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:508/w:460/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTEzOTgt/MTY4MTU1OTk1NS0y/MzIzLmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/rGw8bsmHHGj9DQTTWI27zDeRdqyAA8zNREtLeFzkEsk/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTEzOTgt/MTY4MTU1OTk1NS0y/MzIzLmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"DJ Zinc, is a DJ from the United Kingdom. Zinc first became known for 1995's \"Super Sharp Shooter\", a hip hop/jungle fusion. He went on to produce drum and bass, breakbeat and more recently house music.. Zinc's career as a DJ and producer, however, stretches quite a ways further back, sketching the steady evolution of hardcore from its house and rave roots through ragga and hip-hop-styled hardstep and beyond. He's worked with a broad range of labels, both respected and virtually unknown, done time on the pirate radio circuit (Impact FM, Format FM, Eruption FM), and DJed as a resident at some of London's most popular underground clubs.\r\n\r\nZinc's first production work came in the form of the eight-volume series Swift and Zinc, released on [a=The Dream Team] / [l=Joker Records]' The [a=Bizzy B]'s Brain Progression imprint in the early '90s. The collaboration came to an end in 1994 when [a=Swift (3)] decided to focus on garage and house (the two still share studio space). Remarkably, \"Super Sharp Shooter\" became Zinc's solo debut, and he's since released a number of popular tracks, although none have scaled the heights of his first. From mid 90ies to mid 2000s he was recording with [a=DJ Hype], [a=Pascal], and [a=Rude Bwoy Monty] as part of [a=The Ganja Kru] (for MCA sublabel [l=Parousia]), as well as for Hype's True Playaz label (as DJ Zinc and Dope Skillz). He also runs [l=Bingo Beats] for breakstep etc material.\r\nZinc's career as a DJ and producer stretches the steady evolution of hardcore from its house and rave roots through ragga and hip hop-styled hardstep and beyond. In 1991 he began his radio show with then partner dj swift on London's impact fm. He continued to DJ on various pirate radio stations, including Rinse fm while it was a pirate, and has continued since it was awarded a license in 2011[2]\r\n\r\nHe continued to DJ on a regular basis, and was one of the first drum and bass producers to score with the 2-step garage market courtesy of his 1999 single, \"138 Trek\".[2]\r\n\r\nHis productions have been relatively popular outside the drum and bass scene, most notably in the breaks genre under the pseudonym Jammin. He reached No. 27 in the UK chart with \"138 Trek\" in November 2000.\r\n\r\nZinc produced and DJ'd as part of the Ganja Kru and True Playaz collectives, often in association with DJ Hype from 1996 to 2002. He ran the Bingo Beats record label, which released both drum and bass and breaks records from 2002 to 2008. He has been known to release records under the alias of Dope Skillz, Jammin and Jelly Jams plus others.\r\n\r\nIn 2001, Zinc released a remix compilation album of his own productions called Beats By Design which included \"138 Trek\". By August 2004, he released his second album, Faster, a concept album of sorts. The album, as the name suggests, ups the tempo little-by-little for each song. The title track and album opener, epitomises this and is a grimy crawl through bass and beat that speeds up from 40bpm into the early 100s by its end.[3]\r\n\r\nIn 2007, Zinc stopped making and playing drum and bass, citing \"disenchantment with the scene\". He started producing again in 2009 with a fusion of deep house, funky house and fidget house, which he believed did not fit into any of these subgenres. He later named his new sound \"crack house\".[4]\r\n\r\nIn October 2009, DJ Zinc released Crack House EP encompassing his new sound of the crack house genre. A follow up to this EP, Crack House Vol. 2 was released in July 2010. In November 2009, DJ Zinc created a 2-hour mix for broadcasting on BBC Radio 1's Essential Mix.\r\n\r\nIn 2011, Zinc collaborated with A-Trak to release \"Stingray\", which also has a \"crack house\" sound. DJ Zinc is also well known for his radio show on Rinse FM.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:1398","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":12921,"name":"Jammin'"},{"id":12922,"name":"Shox"},{"id":12930,"name":"Tuned Air"},{"id":31768,"name":"Dope Skillz"},{"id":44871,"name":"Pacific"},{"id":63345,"name":"Zenith (2)"},{"id":73422,"name":"Swoopes"},{"id":174910,"name":"Jack Ruby"},{"id":212921,"name":"Tyranny"},{"id":422576,"name":"Subreption"},{"id":590027,"name":"The Phantom (4)"},{"id":1470036,"name":"Benjamin Pettit"},{"id":3453960,"name":"Shelltoe (2)"}],"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":["D.J. Zink","DJ Zine","DJ Zink","Dj Zinc","Z","Zinc","Zinc Beats","Zync"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/1398-DJ-Zinc","type":"artist","urls":["http://www.djzinc.com","https://myspace.com/bingozinc","http://soundcloud.com/zinc","http://twitter.com/djzinc","http://www.youtube.com/user/djzinc","https://www.youtube.com/@djzincofficial","https://djzinc.bandcamp.com/"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/2116/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"2116","image":"https://i.discogs.com/HA_Lq5Kgzqo9PWxY9pR5mp-Bx4ed3gwE3gM6JM8on-I/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:500/w:500/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTIxMTYt/MTU2NDY2MTI5MS0y/ODM0LmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/MKV2UDwoTlCGU4fDqh6bl3EAjrejhs640vUrm0UUUuQ/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTIxMTYt/MTU2NDY2MTI5MS0y/ODM0LmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"Jungle and Drum n Bass producer/remixer and DJ.\r\n\r\nBrother of [a=Steve White (5)].","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:2116","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":400297,"name":"Monochrome (6)"},{"id":475005,"name":"Darren White (2)"},{"id":2438177,"name":"Velvit"}],"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":["D Bridge","D'Bridge","D-Bridge BadCompanyUK","D. Bridge","D.Bridge","D:Bridge","DBRIDGE","DBridge","Dbridge","d.Bridge","dBRIDGE","dBridge","dbridge"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/2116-D-Bridge","type":"artist","urls":["https://dbridge.bandcamp.com/","http://facebook.com/dbridgemusic","http://instagram.com/dbridge","http://soundcloud.com/dbridge_exitrecords","http://twitter.com/dBridge","http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DBridge"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/278166/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"278166","image":"https://i.discogs.com/lohBxmVRTffkA738sz2W0P-JAbCXaeX6t3Ocn7Hn50Q/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:511/w:398/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTI3ODE2/Ni0xNTI4